(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_structuralorganisationinanimals=[{id:"neet-2026r-q096",pn:96,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog respiration",q:"In water, frogs respire using _____________.",o:["buccal cavity","lungs","trachea","skin"],c:3,s:`Frogs respire differently on land and in water.
In water, the skin acts as the respiratory organ (cutaneous respiration).
Dissolved oxygen in water is exchanged through the skin by diffusion.
On land, the buccal cavity, skin and lungs act as respiratory organs.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q128",pn:128,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog anatomy",q:"In frogs, the number of pairs of cranial nerves arising from the brain are ______.",o:["9","10","12","6"],c:1,s:"In frogs, ten pairs of cranial nerves arise from the brain."},{id:"neet-2026-q164",pn:164,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog: sexual dimorphism",q:`Male frogs can be distinguished from female frogs due to the presence of
A. Bulging eyes
B. Vocal sacs
C. Webbed digits in feet
D. Copulatory pad on first digit of fore limbs
E. Olive green-coloured skin with dark irregular spots`,o:["B and D only","B and C only","A and B only","C and E only"],c:0,s:`Male frogs can be told from females by the vocal sacs and the copulatory pad on the first digit of the fore limbs.
Bulging eyes, webbed digits in feet and olive green skin with dark irregular spots are common in both male and female frogs.`},{id:"neet-2026-q166",pn:166,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog: anatomy",q:`Choose the correct statements regarding frog's anatomy:
A. Hepatic portal system is the special venous connection between liver and intestine.
B. There are twelve pairs of cranial nerves arising from the brain.
C. The ureters and oviducts open separately into the cloaca in female frogs.
D. Hind-brain consists of cerebellum, medulla oblongata and optic lobes.
E. Sinus venosus joins the right atrium of heart.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B and D only","A, C and E only","A, B and C only","B and C only"],c:1,s:`A is correct: in frogs the special venous connection between liver and intestine is the hepatic portal system.
B is incorrect: there are ten pairs of cranial nerves arising from the brain of a frog.
C is correct: in female frogs the ureters (uterus) and oviduct open separately into the cloaca.
D is incorrect: in frogs the mid-brain consists of the optic lobes. The hind-brain consists of the cerebellum and medulla oblongata.
E is correct: in frogs a triangular structure called the sinus venosus joins the right atrium.
Thus the correct statements are A, C and E.`},{id:"neet-2025-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog heart and blood vessels",q:"What is the name of the blood vessel that carries deoxygenated blood from the body to the heart in a frog?",o:["Aorta","Pulmonary artery","Pulmonary vein","Vena cava"],c:3,s:`The frog's heart is a muscular structure with three chambers.
It receives deoxygenated blood from body parts through the major veins called vena cava.
Aorta and pulmonary vein carry oxygenated blood.
The pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood towards the lungs.`},{id:"neet-2025-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog sexual dimorphism",q:"Which of the following statement is correct about location of the male frog copulatory pad?",o:["First and Second digit of fore limb","First digit of hind limb","Second digit of fore limb","First digit of the fore limb"],c:3,s:`In male frogs, the copulatory pad is present on the first digit of the forelimbs.
It is absent in female frogs.`},{id:"neet-2025-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog circulation",q:"In frog, the Renal portal system is a special venous connection that acts to link:",o:["Liver and intestine","Liver and kidney","Kidney and intestine","Kidney and lower part of body"],c:3,s:`Frogs have a special venous connection between liver and intestine, and another between kidney and lower parts of the body.
The first is the hepatic portal system.
The second is the renal portal system.`},{id:"neet-2025-q175",pn:175,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog respiration",q:`Frogs respire in water by skin and buccal cavity and on land by skin, buccal cavity and lungs.
Choose the correct answer from the following:`,o:["The statement is true for water but false for land","The statement is true for both the environment","The statement is false for water but true for land","The statement is false for both the environment"],c:2,s:`In water, frogs respire through the skin and not through the buccal cavity, i.e., only cutaneous respiration.
On land, the buccal cavity, skin and lungs act as respiratory organs, i.e., buccopharyngeal, cutaneous and pulmonary respiration.`},{id:"neet-2024-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach morphology",q:"In both sexes of cockroach, a pair of jointed filamentous structures called anal cerci are present on:",o:["8th and 9th segment","11th segment","5th segment","10th segment"],c:3,s:`In both sexes, the 10th abdominal segment bears a pair of jointed filamentous anal cerci.
The 5th, 8th and 9th segments do not bear them.
The 11th abdominal segment is absent in adult cockroaches.`},{id:"neet-2024-q192",pn:192,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Epithelial tissue",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Unicellular glandular epithelium \u2014 I. Salivary glands
B. Compound epithelium \u2014 II. Pancreas
C. Multicellular glandular epithelium \u2014 III. Goblet cells of alimentary canal
D. Endocrine glandular epithelium \u2014 IV. Moist surface of buccal cavity
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],c:0,s:`Unicellular glandular epithelium \u2013 III. Goblet cells of alimentary canal.
Compound epithelium \u2013 IV. Moist surface of buccal cavity.
Multicellular glandular epithelium \u2013 I. Salivary glands.
Endocrine glandular epithelium \u2013 II. Pancreas.`},{id:"neet-2024-q194",pn:194,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach digestive system",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. The structures used for storing of food. \u2014 I. Gizzard
B. Ring of 6-8 blind tubules at junction of foregut and midgut. \u2014 II. Gastric Caeca
C. Ring of 100-150 yellow coloured thin filaments at junction of midgut and hindgut. \u2014 III. Malpighian tubules
D. The structures used for grinding the food. \u2014 IV. Crop
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],c:2,s:`Structure for grinding food \u2013 Gizzard.
Structure for storing food \u2013 Crop.
Ring of 6-8 blind tubules at foregut-midgut junction \u2013 Gastric caeca; they help secrete digestive juices.
Ring of 100-150 yellow thin filaments at midgut-hindgut junction \u2013 Malpighian tubules; they remove nitrogenous wastes.
This gives A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I.
Note: The printed answer is [3]; the printed solution says option (1), a misprint, and its own matching gives option (3).`},{id:"neet-2023-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Connective tissues",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Ligaments are dense irregular tissue.
Statement II: Cartilage is dense regular tissue.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false."],c:2,s:`Ligament is dense regular connective tissue, so Statement I is false.
Cartilage is a specialised connective tissue, not dense regular tissue, so Statement II is false.`},{id:"neet-2023-q186",pn:186,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Epithelial and connective tissues",q:`Match List I with List II.
List I: A. Mast cells; B. Inner surface of bronchiole; C. Blood; D. Tubular parts of nephron
List II: I. Ciliated epithelium; II. Areolar connective tissue; III. Cuboidal epithelium; IV. Specialised connective tissue
Choose the correct answer from the options give below:`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],c:3,s:`Areolar connective tissue contains fibroblasts, macrophages and mast cells, so A-II.
Bronchioles are lined by ciliated epithelium, so B-I.
Blood is a specialised connective tissue, so C-IV.
Tubular parts of the nephron are lined by cuboidal epithelium, so D-III.`},{id:"neet-2023-q191",pn:191,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach: sexual dimorphism",q:"Which of the following is characteristic feature of cockroach regarding sexual dimorphism?",o:["Presence of anal cerci","Dark brown body colour and anal cerci","Presence of anal styles","Presence of sclerites"],c:2,s:`Anal styles are present only in male cockroaches.
Anal cerci, sclerites and dark brown colour are found in both sexes.`},{id:"neet-2023-q193",pn:193,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach: excretion",q:`In cockroach, excretion is brought about by-
A. Phallic gland
B. Urecose gland
C. Nephrocytes
D. Fat body
E. Collaterial glands
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["B and D only","A and E only","A, B and E only","B, C and D only"],c:3,s:`Cockroach excretion is by Malpighian tubules, fat body, nephrocytes and urecose glands.
Urecose glands make uric acid. Nephrocytes are binucleate cells on the dorsal diaphragm. Fat body stores uric acid.
Phallic gland (male) makes the outer layer of the spermatophore. Collaterial gland (female) makes the ootheca.
So B, C and D.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q152",pn:152,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Epithelial tissue types",q:"Which of the following types of epithelium is present in the bronchioles and Fallopian tubes?",o:["Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium","Ciliated epithelium"],c:3,s:`Ciliated epithelium lines the inner surface of hollow organs.
Bronchioles and Fallopian tubes are such organs.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q158",pn:158,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Muscular tissue types",q:"Choose the correct statement about a muscular tissue:",o:["Smooth muscles are multinucleated and involuntary.","Skeletal muscle fibres are uninucleated and found in parallel bundles.","Intercalated discs allow the cardiac muscle cells to contract as a unit.","The walls of blood vessels are made up of columnar epithelium."],c:2,s:`Smooth muscles have one nucleus per cell and are involuntary, so (1) is wrong.
Skeletal muscle fibres are multinucleated, so (2) is wrong.
Gap junctions in the intercalated discs of cardiac muscle let the cells contract as a unit, so (3) is correct.
Blood vessel walls are lined by simple squamous epithelium, so (4) is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2022-q171",pn:171,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Animal tissues",q:"Which of the following is not a connective tissue?",o:["Adipose tissue","Cartilage","Neuroglia","Blood"],c:2,s:`Neuroglia are supportive cells of nervous tissue, so they are not connective tissue.
Cartilage and blood are specialised connective tissues.
Adipose tissue is a loose connective tissue.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q172",pn:172,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach digestive and excretory organs",q:`Match List - I with List - Il regarding the organs of Cockroach :
      List - I                        List - II
      (a) Crop                        (i) grinding the food particles
      (b) Proventriculus              (ii) secretion of digestive juice
      (c) Hepatic caecae              (iii) removal of nitrogenous waste
      (d) Malpighian tubules (iv) storage of food tubules
      Choose the correct answer from the options given below .`,o:["(a) - (i), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d) - (ii)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (i), (d) - (iii)"],c:1,s:`Crop stores food.
Proventriculus grinds food.
Hepatic caecae secrete digestive juice.
Malpighian tubules remove excretory waste.`},{id:"neet-2022-q177",pn:177,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach external features",q:"Tegmina in cockroach, arises from",o:["Mesothorax","Metathorax","Prothorax and Mesothorax","Prothorax"],c:0,s:`Tegmina are the forewings (first pair) and they arise from the mesothorax.
Hindwings arise from the metathorax.
The prothorax bears no wings.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q177",pn:177,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Animal tissues",q:`Choose the correct statements :
(a) Bones support and protect softer tissues and organs
(b) Weight bearing function is served by limb bones
(c) Ligament is the site of production of blood
(d) Adipose tissue is specialised to store fats.
(e) Tendons attach one bone to another.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below`,o:["(a), (b) and (e) only","(a), (b) and (d) only","(b), (c) and (e) only","(a), (c) and (d) only"],c:1,s:`(c) is false: blood cells are made in bone marrow, not in ligament.
(e) is false: ligaments join bone to bone; tendons join muscle to bone.
(a), (b) and (d) are true.`},{id:"neet-2022-q193",pn:193,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Animal tissues",q:"Match List \u2013 I with List - II (a) Bronchioles (b) Goblet Cell (c) Tendons (d) Adipose Tissue ; (i) Dense Regular Connective tissue (ii) Loose Connective Tissue (iii) Glandular Tissue (iv) Ciliated Epithelium. Choose the Correct answer from the options given below:",o:["(a) - (i), (b) - (ii), (c) - (iii), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (i), (c) - (iv), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (i), (d) - (ii)"],c:3,s:`Bronchioles are lined by ciliated epithelium.
Goblet cells are unicellular glands, so glandular tissue.
Tendons are dense regular connective tissue joining muscle to bone.
Adipose tissue is loose connective tissue storing fat.
So a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q195",pn:195,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach: excretion",q:"Excretion in cockroach is performed by all, EXCEPT:",o:["Hepatic caeca","Urecose glands","Malpighian tubules","Fat body"],c:0,s:`Hepatic (gastric) caeca are 6-8 blind tubules at the foregut-midgut junction.
They secrete digestive juice, not excretion.`},{id:"neet-2021-q152",pn:152,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach anatomy",q:"Which of the following characteristics is incorrect with respect to cockroach?",o:["10th abdominal segment in both sexes, bears a pair of anal cerci","A ring of gastric caeca is present at the junction of midgut and hind gut","Hypopharynx lies within the cavity enclosed by the mouth parts","In females, 7th - 9th sterna together form a genital pouch"],c:1,s:`Gastric caeca form a ring at the junction of foregut and midgut, not midgut and hindgut.
At the midgut-hindgut junction there are Malpighian tubules.
The other three statements are correct.`},{id:"neet-2021-q197",pn:197,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Earthworm",q:`Following are the statements about prostomium of earthworm.
(a) It serves as a covering for mouth.
(b) It helps to open cracks in the soil into which it can crawl.
(c) It is one of the sensory structures.
(d) It is the first body segment.`,o:["(b) and (c) are correct","(a), (b) and (c) are correct","(a), (b) and (d) are correct","(a), (b), (c) and (d) are correct"],c:1,s:`The prostomium is a lobe over the mouth: (a) right.
It acts as a wedge to open soil cracks: (b) right.
It has receptors, so it is sensory: (c) right.
It is not a segment; the first segment is the peristomium: (d) wrong.
So (a), (b) and (c).`},{id:"neet-2021-q200",pn:200,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cell junctions",q:"Identify the types of cell junctions that help to stop the leakage of the substances across a tissue and facilitation of communication with neighbouring cells via rapid transfer of ions and molecules.",o:["Adhering junctions and Gap junctions, respectively","Gap junctions and Adhering junctions, respectively","Tight junctions and Gap junctions, respectively","Adhering junctions and Tight junctions, respectively."],c:2,s:`Tight junctions seal cells together so substances cannot leak across a tissue.
Gap junctions link the cytoplasm of neighbouring cells so ions and small molecules pass quickly.
Adhering junctions only hold cells together.
So tight junctions, then gap junctions.`},{id:"neet-2020-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Epithelial tissue",q:"Cuboidal epithelium with brush border of microvilli is found in :",o:["ducts of salivary glands","proximal convoluted tubule of nephron","eustachian tube","lining of intestine"],c:1,s:"Cuboidal epithelium with a brush border of microvilli lines the proximal convoluted tubule of the nephron."},{id:"neet-2020-q050",pn:50,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach nervous system",q:"If the head of cockroach is removed, it may live for few days because:",o:["the cockroach does not have nervous system.","the head holds a small proportion of a nervous system while the rest is situated along the ventral part of its body.","the head holds a 1/3rd of a nervous system while the rest is situated along the dorsal part of its body.","the supra-oesophageal ganglia of the cockroach are situated in ventral part of abdomen."],c:1,s:`Only a small part of the nervous system (supra-oesophageal ganglia) is in the head.
The rest is along the ventral side of the body, so the body lives on for some days.`},{id:"neet-2020-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Epithelial tissue",q:"Goblet cells of alimentary canal are modified from :",o:["Columnar epithelial cells","Chondrocytes","Compound epithelial cells","Squamous epithelial cells"],c:0,s:"Goblet cells that secrete mucus are modified columnar epithelial cells."},{id:"neet-2019-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Epithelial tissue",q:"The ciliated epithelial cells are required to move particles or mucus in a specific direction. In humans, these cells are mainly present in :",o:["Eustachian tube and Salivary duct","Bronchioles and Fallopian tubes","Bile duct and Bronchioles","Fallopian tubes and Pancreatic duct"],c:1,s:`Ciliated epithelium lines the bronchioles, where it moves mucus.
It also lines the fallopian tubes, where it moves the ovum.
So the answer is bronchioles and fallopian tubes.`},{id:"neet-2019-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach",q:"Select the correct sequence of organs in the alimentary canal of cockroach starting from mouth :",o:["Pharynx \u2192 Oesophagus \u2192 Gizzard \u2192 Ileum \u2192 Crop \u2192 Colon \u2192 Rectum","Pharynx \u2192 Oesophagus \u2192 Ileum \u2192 Crop \u2192 Gizzard \u2192 Colon \u2192 Rectum","Pharynx \u2192 Oesophagus \u2192 Crop \u2192 Gizzard \u2192 Ileum \u2192 Colon \u2192 Rectum","Pharynx \u2192 Oesophagus \u2192 Gizzard \u2192 Crop \u2192 Ileum \u2192 Colon \u2192 Rectum"],c:2,s:`Food goes from the mouth to pharynx and oesophagus.
Then it goes to the crop (storage) and the gizzard (grinding).
Then it passes through the ileum and colon to the rectum.`}]})();
